-- TWO NAMED-FIELD CONSTRUCTORS OF ONE TYPE — the axis every cell in this corpus was
-- missing until #1455's adversarial review (finding 1).  The corpus varied field ORDER
-- and module IDENTITY; every type in it had at most ONE named-field constructor, so no
-- input could reach the case where two `RecordInfo`s share a type identity.
--
-- 🚨 A `.field` SELECTION THROUGH SUCH A TYPE IS NOT IN THIS CELL, AND ITS ABSENCE IS
-- THE FINDING.  The first version of this cell selected `mkA.px` on a `Pair` whose two
-- constructors AGREE on every offset, on the argument that an agreeing layout makes the
-- stamp immaterial.  That argument is true of the LLVM backend and false of WasmGC, and
-- `gates (engines)` is what said so: on WasmGC a record is a `struct` type PER
-- CONSTRUCTOR, so the stamp is a CAST, and casting a `PB` to `PA`'s struct type is
-- illegal whatever the layouts are.  MEASURED on cold builds of BOTH the merge base
-- `bfcd4ea7` and this branch — so it is pre-existing, not introduced:
--
--     shape                                          native      WasmGC
--     |PA{px,py}|PB{px,py,pz}| , mkA.px and mkB.px    pa=1,2 …    trap: illegal cast
--     |PA{px,py}|PB{px,py}|    , equal arity too      p=1,3       trap: illegal cast
--     |A{x}|B{x}|              , IDENTICAL fields     t=1,2       trap: illegal cast
--     …the same, with only the STAMPED ctor selected  t=1         t=1        (ok)
--
-- So such a selection is now refused by `stampableFieldRecord`
-- (`T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT`) and this cell exercises the remedy the diagnostic names
-- — `match` — which every engine agrees on.  The rejection itself is pinned where a
-- rejection belongs, in `test/typecheck_error_fixtures/field_variant_*`.
public export data Pair =
  | PA { px : Int, py : Int }
  | PB { px : Int, py : Int, pz : Int }

export mkA : Pair
mkA = PA { px = 1, py = 2 }

export mkB : Pair
mkB = PB { px = 3, py = 4, pz = 5 }

export pairPx : Pair -> Int
pairPx p = match p
  PA { px = x } => x
  PB { px = x } => x

-- 🚨 THE BYSTANDER.  `Wrap`'s two constructors DISAGREE on `alpha`'s offset (0 in
-- `Cfg`, 1 in `Dfg`).  Declaring such a type is still legal, and BOTH of its
-- constructors are constructed here — which is what puts them in the emitter's
-- construction-site-built `recName -> labels` table, the discriminator #1455's reviewer
-- isolated.  Nothing in the entry's output comes from a `.alpha`, so this cell asserts
-- about code that has NOTHING TO DO with the conflicting declaration: `AGENTS.md`'s
-- "feature present, UNRELATED code still behaves" shape, which is what a program-wide
-- rule owes and what a "does my feature work" fixture cannot give.
public export data Wrap =
  | Cfg { alpha : Int, beta : Int }
  | Dfg { beta : Int, alpha : Int }

export mkC : Wrap
mkC = Cfg { alpha = 10, beta = 20 }

export mkD : Wrap
mkD = Dfg { beta = 220, alpha = 110 }

-- ⚠️ BOTH ARMS RETURN THE SAME PROJECTION ON PURPOSE, and that is a guard, not
-- laziness.  `Cfg` and `Dfg` share BOTH labels, and an arm naming only shared labels
-- picks the arm by CONSTRUCTOR natively but by LABEL SET in the interpreter (#1462,
-- re-measured on the merge base while building this cell).  What is NOT divergent is
-- the BINDING: the interpreter still binds `alpha` by name.  Making both arms project
-- `alpha` therefore keeps this cell a test of THIS defect rather than of #1462.
export wrapAlpha : Wrap -> Int
wrapAlpha w = match w
  Cfg { alpha = a } => a
  Dfg { alpha = a } => a

-- The SINGLE-constructor half, kept so this cell still asserts a VALUE that depends on
-- the stamp `#1455` changed.  Two records, one named-field constructor each, sharing
-- both field names at OPPOSITE offsets: `mkS.sx` and `mkO.sx` can only both be right if
-- each selection resolved to its OWN declaration.  A single-constructor type is exactly
-- the case the guard leaves alone — the stamp IS the runtime constructor, always.
public export data Solo = { sx : Int, sy : Int }

export mkS : Solo
mkS = Solo { sx = 7, sy = 8 }

public export data Olos = { sy : Int, sx : Int }

export mkO : Olos
mkO = Olos { sy = 6, sx = 5 }
